 was  or if they'd ever be reunited it's all  right here  lipman also says the country isolation  central to the 2018  horror film a quiet place influenced her  thinking  i've always wanted to write something  horror-tinged in a city  where you can see people and people  maybe can see you  and where you have your phone and the  electricity works  and you're still so isolated because i  felt that people were more isolated than  they knew  dream girl is it's it's crime it's  detective it's mystery  it's horror it's all those  yeah throw in literary fiction while  you're at it yeah sure  absolutely and it certainly takes some  um  i don't want to spoil anything but it  takes some pretty severe  harsh turns yes well if i write a book a  body's going to drop  you kill people i kill people that's  what i do for a living  lipman's last novel the critically  acclaimed  bestseller lady in the lake began with  another body  inspired by two real murderers in  baltimore in the 1960s it follows a  white reporter as she tries to solve the  murder of a young black woman during  that same era  you wrote about race a lot obviously in  lady in the lake  different than dream girl why why did  you feel like you needed to do that  i live in baltimore if i don't write  about race sometime  i'm not writing about baltimore but do  you think lady in the lake was so good  because you forced yourself to do  something different  yeah i've tried very hard never to write  the same book twice  and you know i'm over 20 books in  and it gets harder eleven years ago  lipman  and her husband fellow writer david  simon welcomed a daughter  she chronicled that in a rare personal  essay titled game of crohn's  republished in her 2020 collection my  life  as a villainous i was 51 when my  daughter was born why did you want to  document all that  i wanted to tell this story because at  the time i thought it was  unique like i don't know anyone  like me i don't know someone who's got  a kid in grade school in his writing  novels and just has sort of this  weird improbable life and what i found  out when i wrote the piece is that it  was far more universal than i ever knew  i felt like when i walked up to the  elementary school to drop my kid off  after that piece was published on the  internet  i mean i really felt like i was just  like you know walking into applause like  woman after woman  came up to me and said oh i so got it  along with  author wife and mom you can also add  mentor littman brings up and coming  writers to baltimore  and gives them a place to live and work  it's wonderful i've never talked about  it publicly and i probably shouldn't  because you cannot apply for the litman  fellowship  i must just reach down from the heavens  and with me and  yeah we're working on a short list of  people the office is under renovation  and i can't reach out to anyone until  i'm really sure when it's going to be  back  in working order well with full  apologies you might be getting some  additional applications  yeah well i opened my mouth so  it happened if you had forecast your  career  in 99 and looked at what you've done now  what would you think  i won't say it's beyond my wildest  dreams because i can dream pretty wildly  but it definitely hit the the upper 99  percentile of what i thought would be  possible if i got  very very very lucky she did get very  lucky and she's also very talented  libman fellowship applications here i  mean yeah watch out now i l  i hate saying i love it but the fact  that she had worked for so long  and then found the success and i know  we've told stories before like that  it just feels like somehow that it it  means something different to you at that  point and also the fact that some  authors do  tend to write the same book over and  over right she always forces herself as  we said to do something different  yeah i love that she's a journalist  proves that  there you go got some talent out there  beyond the talent we have that'